From d0e64ac80c46def3be5c4c1d5206d8012b7347c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:56:47 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 23/32] super-amd: read AMD RAIDXpert2 (RAIDCore) metadata

AMD RAIDXpert2 is the RAID of AMD chipsets (X399, X570, X670, X870...):
the BIOS (RAIDCoreDxe) and the AMD Windows driver keep the arrays in
undocumented metadata, and the AMD Linux driver is an out-of-tree binary
module.  This and the following patches add a handler for it, so that the
kernel's md runs these arrays, with mdmon keeping their state where the
firmware and Windows expect it - as for IMSM.

The format was worked out for interoperability from the metadata on our
own disks and a published dump, and from our own board's firmware: a
label at LBA 0x5000, a commit
block at 0x5001 naming the live configuration packet in a ring at
0x5800-0x5fff; the packet lists the disks (PD records) and the volumes
(LD records, with their elements) of the whole system, under a 64-bit
checksum.  Each disk is a member of the container; each LD is a volume.

This first part reads the metadata:

- --examine, --detail, and assembly (-A, -I) of every volume the AMD BIOS
  or driver made: Volume (linear), RAID0, RAID1, RAID10, RAID5 (md raid5
  right-asymmetric), and RAIDABLE - one disk kept for a future mirror -
  as an md raid1 of one member;
- the configuration is the newest one of the disks the AMD driver would
  take it from (the disks last online on its own controller); a disk
  that missed configurations is stale: a RAID1, RAID10 or RAID5 runs
  without it (with --run), while a Volume, RAID0 or
  RAIDABLE runs with it if the disk's own configuration has the volume
  in the same place - it cannot have been written without the disk;
- AMD records no ID of a disk set: a disk belongs to the container whose
  live configuration lists it and whose disks its own configuration lists
  - so a stale disk joins its container, and a disk taken into another
  set does not join the old one;
- of two disks with one DeviceID (a member copied with dd, both
  attached) the one with the newer configuration is taken, and mdadm
  says which disk is left out;
- Volume and RAID0 run read-write: AMD records no state of them that md
  changes.  The other levels start read-only, until mdmon records their
  state (next patch); nothing is written to the disks yet.

A volume md cannot run as AMD lays it out is not started (RAID50, a
distributed spare, a migration in progress), nor one whose member
extents are not where AMD puts them (past the metadata, a service area
in front of the data, within the disk): mdmon writes its logs there.
AMD has RAID6 in its code but no platform enables it.

The handler as a whole (this and the following patches) was tested on an
AMD X870E board with SATA HDDs and SSDs (the BIOS menu takes what mdadm
wrote; the board boots Linux from RAID1 and RAID5 volumes; the AMD
Windows and Linux drivers read and write mdadm's RAID0 and RAID1 there),
and in emulation: the
board's RAIDCoreDxe in QEMU with SATA and NVMe disks, and the AMD Windows
driver in a VM on disk images; the transform log both ways with the AMD
Linux driver (rcraid) in a QEMU guest.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
